.he "stty (1)                     9/30/26                     stty (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
stty
- set typewriter (terminal) options
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
stty [ -a ] [ option ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Stty
sets the input and output options of the typewriter attached to its
standard output, and reports them.
.sp
With no option it reports the settings which differ from the ones a
login line is given when it is opened, as one line on the standard
error.  An option in the report is named as the keyword which would
undo it: the report says
.bd
tabs
when tabs are preserved, and
.bd
-tabs
when they are replaced by spaces.
.sp
Any number of options may be given, and they are applied in order.
One that is not an option is named on the standard error, and nothing
is set.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-a	Report every option, with the sign it is in, instead of
only those which differ from the default.

.ti -10
raw	Raw mode input: no erase, kill, interrupt, quit or end of
file processing, and the parity bit is passed.

.ti -10
-raw	Negate raw mode.

.ti -10
cooked	Same as -raw.  Cooked mode is the default.

.ti -10
-nl	Allow carriage return for new-line, and output CR-LF for
carriage return or new-line.  This is the default.

.ti -10
nl	Accept only new-line to end lines.

.ti -10
echo	Echo back every character typed.  Echo is asserted by
default.

.ti -10
-echo	Do not echo characters.

.ti -10
lcase	Map upper case to lower case.

.ti -10
-lcase	Do not map case.  This is the default.

.ti -10
-tabs	Replace tabs by spaces when printing.  This is the
default.

.ti -10
tabs	Preserve tabs.

.ti -10
shake	Do hardware handshaking, using the clear-to-send (RS-232
CTS) pin.

.ti -10
-shake	No handshaking.  This is the default.

.ti -10
data8	Keep all 8 bits of input data.

.ti -10
-data8	Keep only 7 bits of input data.  This is the default.

.ti -10
data7	Same as -data8; keep only 7 bits of input data.

.ti -10
erase c	Set the erase character to c.  A c written ^x, where x
is any character, is the control character x.  The default erase
character is ^H.

.ti -10
kill c	Set the kill character to c, written the same way.  The
default kill character is ^X.

.in -10
.sp
A baud rate may be named instead of a keyword, and it sets both the
input and the output speed of the line:
.sp
.nf
	50 75 110 134 134.5 150 200 300 600
	1200 1800 2400 4800 9600 19200
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The report is one line, and the speed in it is "?" when the line does
not have a rate this command knows: a terminal the driver has never
been told a speed for answers with a code of its own.  The erase and
kill characters are always reported, as there is no default for them
to differ from.
.sp
Micronix presently has no built-in delays, so the delay keywords the
1982 stty has - cr0 to cr3, nl0 to nl3, tab0 to tab3, ff0, ff1, bs0,
bs1 - are not here.  They are also not free: stty (2) gives bs1 and
shake the one word 0100000 and ff1 and the 8-bit input bit the one
word 040000.
.sp
The terminal driver does not map case, so lcase and -lcase set the
bit and change nothing else.
.sp
-a is not on the 1982 page; it is what the stty on the distribution
disk takes, and it is the only way to see the options that are at
their default.
.sp
The erase and kill characters may be written ^x, which is v7's form
and the form the defaults are written in above; the stty on the
distribution disk takes the character literally.  That stty also has
the sense of tabs the other way round, so that a user following this
page and a user of the disk's command ask for opposite things.  This
command follows the page, and stty (2), and the driver.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
stty (2), gtty (2), login (1), init (8)
